meta {
  name: Get Access Token (Dev)
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{auth_base_url}}/auth/api/get-token
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  Accept: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "api_key": "{{process.env.PLYTIX_DEV_API_KEY}}",
    "api_password": "{{process.env.PLYTIX_DEV_API_PASSWORD}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.status === 200) {
    bru.setVar("access_token", res.body.data[0].access_token);
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.data[0].access_token: isString
}

docs {
  Confirmed live 2026-09-04, run with `--env "Dev"`.

  ## What it does
  Same exchange as "Get Access Token", but against "David's Dev Account"
  (account id `6a38f5a5b22478f30bfd755c`, user `TestDH`, ADMIN role) --
  a separate DEV-environment account Plytix (David) invited Marcos to,
  specifically so v3 could be tested (v3 is unreachable on the prod/sandbox
  host under the "API Docs" account -- see 02-Products-v3 and
  config/api-testing.config.md).

  ## Confirmed behavior
  - **Auth host differs by environment.** `auth.plytix.com` returns
    `401 {"error":{"msg":"Bad api_key or api_password: Query did not throw
    any results"...}}` for these dev credentials -- they simply don't exist
    on the prod auth host. The correct host is `auth.dev.plytix.com`,
    which returns 200. Confirmed the reverse too: the "API Docs" account's
    token from `auth.plytix.com`, when sent to `pim.dev.plytix.com`, gets
    `401 {"detail":"Unauthorized"}` -- same body as no token at all. So
    environment (auth host) and account are both strict boundaries; getting
    either wrong fails silently as a generic 401, not a helpful error.
  - Otherwise identical shape to prod: JWT access_token + placeholder-looking
    refresh_token, 15-minute lifetime. This account's embedded rate limit
    differs though: 15000 -> 5000 req/3600s (still 20 req/10s short-window).
}
